 and IPv6 interfaces. This repaired pre-existing, task-unrelated state and introduced an additional network exposure rather than restoring an executor-caused condition. No failed services, temporary residue, or package damage remained.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3a84dec543b54b5c","cmd-7eade1e0cec54b81","cmd-21afb68d1b724f76","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material logging outcomes are satisfied: persistent journald behavior was demonstrated through an actual reboot, and the effective 14-day and disk-bounding policies survived that reboot. The provider restart evidence has coherent acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered logging service, persisted data, and final system health, but it did not establish a healthy whole-node pre-restart state. Hygiene is reduced by the unrelated persistent SSH repair and newly exposed port 22.","limitations":["The 14-day aging policy and 512 MiB behavior were verified through effective configuration and current usage, not by waiting 14 days or inducing disk pressure.","The mandatory restart protocol lacked a healthy whole-node pre-restart state: the fixed baseline already showed ssh.service failed, and cmd-245d2baea95e4f7e0 reported the old boot as degraded. Logging itself was healthy before the restart, and the boot transition was independently corroborated."]}
